Example 1: Two were killed and one injured.

❌ Incorrect as written.

Break it down: "Two were killed" + "one [???] injured"
The missing word for "one" should logically be "was" (singular, matching "one") — but the only verb given in the sentence is "were" (plural, matching "two"). 
You can't reuse "were" for "one" — that's a mismatch.
✅ Two were killed and one was injured. (spell out "was" — don't try to share "were")
Example 2: One was killed and two injured.

❌ Same problem, reversed.

"One was killed" + "two [???] injured" → the missing word for "two" should be "were," not "was."
✅ One was killed and two were injured.
Example 3: He has and will help the poor.

❌ Incorrect.

"He has [help/helped] the poor" + "he will help the poor"
"Has" needs a past participle after it (has helped), but "will" needs the base form (will help). 
You can't share the single word "help" for both — "has help" is wrong.
✅ He has helped and will help the poor. (spell out both forms separately)

Example 4: His assistants have and are still doing great job.

❌ Incorrect.

"have [done]" (past participle needed) + "are doing" (present participle) — again, two different verb forms are needed, so you can't share just "doing."
✅ His assistants have done and are still doing a great job.



The Core Rule

When a word is omitted because it's assumed to be "shared" between two clauses, 
check that the omitted word is grammatically identical in form for BOTH clauses.
If the required form is different in each clause (was vs. were, helped vs. help, done vs. doing), 
you cannot omit it — you must write both forms out.
